WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.900
Every neuron here looks finished.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.440
Which ones were born first?

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.560
An adult brain carries no date stamps on its cells.

4
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:14.800
To learn when cells were born, biologists turn DNA copying itself into a clock.

5
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:21.943
A dividing cell copies its DNA once per division, in S phase.

6
00:00:23.303 --> 00:00:27.543
A neuron that stopped dividing normally does not copy its genome again.

7
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:33.983
Biologists give a brief pulse of a thymidine analog, a labelled DNA building block.

8
00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:40.203
Because thymidine goes only into DNA, every cell in S phase builds it in.

9
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:45.203
Next comes the chase: the label is gone, and time moves on.

10
00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:51.283
A labelled progenitor that divides one last time passes heavy label to two

11
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:57.283
daughters. Those newborn neurons never divide again, so they stay heavily labelled

12
00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:03.183
for life. Progenitors that keep dividing split that label roughly in half each

13
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:04.183
division.

14
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:09.437
Heavy label therefore marks an approximate last S phase, when the cell stopped

15
00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:15.557
dividing. In the cortical plate, earlier birthdays settle deeper, and younger ones

16
00:01:15.557 --> 00:01:22.677
sit higher. Early work used radioactive tritiated thymidine, followed later by BrdU.

17
00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:29.117
A newer mimic, EdU, is detected by click chemistry, which attaches a fluorescent

18
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:30.117
tag.

19
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.370
Still, a glowing nucleus alone can mislead.

20
00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.510
Picture an injured tissue whose cells are thought to be non-dividing.

21
00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.150
After a pulse, a BrdU-positive nucleus appears.

22
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.290
Must that cell have passed through S phase?

23
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:58.273
No. Repair also builds short new DNA stretches that analogs can enter.

24
00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:05.253
In injured tissue, BrdU alone cannot separate repair from replication without

25
00:02:05.253 --> 00:02:06.593
cell-cycle evidence.

26
00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:09.933
Nor is a birth record a cell identity.

27
00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:16.293
Under a light microscope, label might belong to a glial cell, a support cell,

28
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:17.553
pressed against a neuron.

29
00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:23.093
Confirming cell type takes an extra check, like electron microscopy.

30
00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.867
That check showed adult brains can make neurons.

31
00:02:28.227 --> 00:02:34.507
In the 1960s, Joseph Altman found labelled cells in rat dentate gyrus and moving

32
00:02:34.507 --> 00:02:35.807
toward the olfactory bulb.

33
00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:43.847
In 1977, Michael Kaplan and James Hinds used electron microscopy to confirm they

34
00:02:43.847 --> 00:02:50.727
were neurons. In 1983, Goldman and Nottebohm tracked new adult canary neurons from

35
00:02:50.727 --> 00:02:53.247
ventricle to song nucleus HVC.

36
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.600
EdU can also separate division speed from daughter fate.

37
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.840
A short pulse roughly measures rate; a chase shows what labelled daughters become.

38
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.880
In 2023, Abby and colleagues tracked mouse clones lacking one or both copies of

39
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.180
Notch1. The clones expanded without dividing faster.

40
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.820
EdU tracking fit more daughters staying progenitors: fate, not speed.

41
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:25.093
Across tissues, the rule holds.

42
00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:31.373
Pair a timed DNA pulse with controls, and heavy label records a last division.

43
00:03:32.513 --> 00:03:35.053
What the cell became still needs its own proof.
